    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Tests the behavior when a texture stage consumes a texture coordinate that the vertex shader never wrote.</para>
<para>This test investigates behavior observed in &quot;Driv3r&quot; <ulink url="https://github.com/xemu-project/xemu/issues/1614">https://github.com/xemu-project/xemu/issues/1614</ulink> The game draws with texture stage 3 in cube map mode, but its vertex shader never writes oT3.</para>
<para>These tests check whether an unwritten texture coordinate output keeps a stale value from previously processed vertices (as is the case for the fog coordinate, see <ref refid="d1/d48/classFogCarryoverTests" kindref="compound">FogCarryoverTests</ref>) or uses a fixed default. The behavior of a cube map lookup with a zero length direction is covered separately by TextureCubemapTests::CubemapZeroVector.</para>
<para>The &quot;Stage&lt;N&gt;*&quot; tests render the texture coordinate directly as a color (texture shader mode PASS_THROUGH, with the combiner outputting TEX&lt;N&gt;), so any carried value is visible without any texture lookup. Each row uses a different submission mode: IB (inline buffer), IE (inline elements), IA (inline array), DA (draw arrays). Quads are used except by PerVertex, which uses points so that no interpolation occurs. &lt;N&gt; is 3 (the Driv3r case) or 0 (to see whether oT0 behaves like oT3).</para>
<para>Observations from HW:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Unwritten texture coordinate outputs do not carry over. In every test, every quad and point drawn with a shader that does not write oT0 or oT3 renders black, regardless of the values written by the preceding draws. Not writing a texture coordinate from the vertex shader results in (0, 0, 0) for that coordinate. </para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
